WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.105
A strong counter-deception review begins by separating three things that often get blended together:

00:00:05.105 --> 00:00:07.608
the claim, the source, and the evidence.

00:00:07.608 --> 00:00:12.613
The claim is what someone says is true or wants the organization to believe.

00:00:12.613 --> 00:00:17.619
The source is where the claim came from, including a person, system, vendor, report,

00:00:17.619 --> 00:00:18.691
account, or channel.

00:00:18.691 --> 00:00:23.697
The evidence is the artifact, telemetry, record, or observation offered to support the claim.

00:00:23.697 --> 00:00:27.630
Reliability and credibility are related, but they are not the same.

00:00:27.630 --> 00:00:31.920
Reliability describes the source over time: does this source usually provide accurate,

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:33.708
complete, and responsibly handled information?

00:00:33.708 --> 00:00:36.925
Credibility describes the specific information in front of you:

00:00:36.925 --> 00:00:39.786
is this claim internally consistent, supported by evidence,

00:00:39.786 --> 00:00:41.931
and compatible with independently observed context?

00:00:41.931 --> 00:00:44.433
A usually reliable source can be wrong,

00:00:44.433 --> 00:00:47.651
and an unfamiliar source can provide information worth checking.

00:00:47.651 --> 00:00:52.657
Provenance is the record of where evidence came from and how it was handled.

00:00:52.657 --> 00:00:57.662
For a log export, provenance may include the system, query, time range, account used,

00:00:57.662 --> 00:00:58.734
and collection method.

00:00:58.734 --> 00:01:03.382
For a screenshot, it may include who captured it, when it was captured,

00:01:03.382 --> 00:01:08.388
what view it came from, and whether the underlying system can still be checked.

00:01:08.388 --> 00:01:12.321
Provenance helps analysts distinguish an original artifact from a copy, summary,

00:01:12.321 --> 00:01:14.466
transformation, or claim about an artifact.

00:01:14.466 --> 00:01:18.398
Corroboration improves confidence when independent sources point in the same direction.

00:01:18.398 --> 00:01:19.114
Independence matters.

00:01:19.114 --> 00:01:23.404
Three messages copied from the same original source do not provide the

00:01:23.404 --> 00:01:25.549
same value as a log record,

00:01:25.549 --> 00:01:30.554
a ticket history, and an approved workflow record that each support the same conclusion.

00:01:30.554 --> 00:01:33.415
By separating claim, source, evidence, provenance, and corroboration,

00:01:33.415 --> 00:01:38.420
analysts can communicate what is solid, what is plausible, and what still needs verification.
